## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical tenant isolation pattern in a single AWS account using core services, focusing on real-world constraints for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation with DynamoDB LeadingKeys and per-tenant partition keys
- IAM policy conditions using ForAllValues:StringEquals and PrincipalTag
- API Gateway/Lambda authorizer to inject TenantId into requests
- Auditing and testability with CloudTrail data events

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["dynamodb:GetItem","dynamodb:PutItem","dynamodb:Query"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:dynamodb:region:acct:table/TenantTable",
      "Condition": {
        "ForAllValues:StringEquals": {
          "dynamodb:LeadingKeys": ["TENANT#${aws:PrincipalTag/TenantId}"]
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate per-tenant keys without downtime?
- How would you test breach scenarios with cross-tenant access attempts?

Why This Is Asked
- Tests a practical, scalable multi-tenant pattern within a single bucket, emphasizing isolation, encryption, and rapid containment.
- Exercises policy design using S3, KMS, and IAM with MFA, plus a real-time breach response workflow.

Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation via per-tenant S3 prefix and IAM role scope
- SSE-KMS with per-tenant CMKs and encryption context validation
- Containment orchestration using EventBridge, Lambda, and CloudTrail

Code Example
```json
// S3 bucket policy (illustrative)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/Tenant123Role"},
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-bucket/tenant-123/*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"s3:prefix": "tenant-123/"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// KMS key policy (illustrative)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/Tenant123Role"},
      "Action": ["kms:Encrypt","kms:Decrypt"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {"kms:EncryptionContext:TenantID": "tenant-123"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you test the 60-second containment workflow end-to-end? 
- How would you extend this to support new tenants without increasing policy complexity?

